{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2020,
  "narrative": "The 2020 Chicago Cubs arrived at their truncated 60-game season with genuine expectations, and their fast start validated the optimism. Finishing July at 5-2, the Cubs leaned on their bullpen and timely pitching to establish early control, with Jeremy Jeffress proving to be a reliable late-inning weapon in those critical opening weeks. The shortened season rewarded teams that could win close games, and Chicago seemed built for exactly that kind of grind.\n\nAugust brought both the season's most electrifying moments and its first real warning signs. David Bote's three-run blast and Kyle Schwarber's clutch homer powered stretches of genuine dominance, but Craig Kimbrel's late-August implosion served as a sobering reminder that the Cubs' closer situation remained a vulnerability. Despite those late stumbles, the team finished August at 15-12 — workmanlike, not spectacular, but enough to keep them squarely in the playoff hunt in a year when half the league would qualify.\n\nSeptember sharpened the stakes and revealed the Cubs' true character. Jason Heyward's massive three-run homer on September 12th was the month's defining highlight, a reminder that even the team's most scrutinized contract could deliver when it mattered most. Javier Báez provided his customary dose of clutch magic, and Kris Bryant's grand slam late in the month felt like a punctuation mark on a regular season that ended 34-26 — good enough to win the NL Central. But the bullpen continued to crater at the worst moments, with Andrew Chafin and Ryan Tepera surrendering leads that the offense worked hard to build.\n\nThe Wild Card round against the Miami Marlins ended the Cubs' season with a harsh two-game sweep, and the postseason clips tell the story plainly. Kyle Hendricks couldn't protect leads, Jeremy Jeffress coughed up another run, and Yu Darvish — the staff ace all year — was punished in Game 2. Ian Happ provided a lone home run bright spot, and the Báez-to-Bote-to-Rizzo double play showed the Cubs' defensive brilliance, but it wasn't enough. A season full of promise and occasional magic closed quietly in October, ending the core group's final run together without a series win.",
  "chapters": [
